WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.940
On today's AI briefing, we think a mandatory limit pushed by the best-funded lab is more likely to raise entry costs than slow the leaders, which helps incumbents with compliance budgets.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:23.820
This is the AI briefing for Monday, November 2. I'm Michael. Like everything here, this briefing is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.660
Today starts with a lab asking for rules on its own field.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:35.400
OpenAI is reportedly planning a push, after the midterms, for mandatory speed limits on powerful AI systems.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.680
It is also weighing a new name for its data centers.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.480
The stake is who those rules would end up costing most.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.360
Then robotics security.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:52.240
VicOne says AI-assisted research turned up 28 zero-day findings in robotics software in under two days.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.200
Some of the projects are developed or maintained by Google, NVIDIA, and Meta.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.980
If AI makes bug hunting that fast, the shared code under many robots becomes a target.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.860
Those are our two main stories, and then a few quick hits,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:13.900
including a large Canadian bet on Cohere and NVIDIA's role in financing an anthropic data center.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.380
First, Stephen's take on the top of the day.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.040
Black market is interesting. We should figure this out.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.920
First story, OpenAI and the politics of slowing down.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:33.000
Axios reports that OpenAI plans a policy campaign after the midterms,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.300
arguing for mandatory speed limits on powerful AI systems.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.180
Separately, TechCrunch reports the company is considering a new name for its data centers.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:48.880
The label under discussion in TechCrunch's telling is AI Compute Campuses.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:50.920
What's new is the pairing.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.200
The best-funded lab would be asking for binding limits on the field it leads,

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.580
while softening the language around the buildings that power it.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.320
How well does this hold up?

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.120
It is reporting, not confirmed action.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.900
Both pieces describe internal planning,

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.660
and nothing has been verified outside the company.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.040
There is no proposal text yet.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.940
Here is where we come down.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.500
We think a mandatory limit pushed by the best-funded lab

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.060
is more likely to raise entry costs than to slow the leaders.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.580
That helps incumbents who already have compliance budgets.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:30.960
The campus rename, as we see it, is about the local politics of land and power.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.280
The strongest case the other way deserves a fair hearing.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.840
A frontier lab backing mandatory limits concedes the risk is real.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.000
That could make binding rules politically viable after the midterms.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.180
And the campus framing may ease local opposition to new builds.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:52.000
The case against starts with the fact that only internal planning is reported.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:01.440
Limits drafted with the leader's input tend to fit the leader and raise costs for smaller labs, and a rebrand changes the label, not the power draw.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:09.260
One question decides it for us. Would the proposed limits constrain OpenAI's own release schedule as much as smaller rivals?

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:17.040
Until the proposal text shows otherwise, we lean toward reading this as moat building by a well-capitalized lab.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.160
For early-stage companies, the split matters.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:28.100
If mandatory limits pass, seed stage model builders could face compliance costs sized for OpenAI.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:34.180
Application companies building on Frontier AP is would carry less of that burden.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.840
A fair question for a model building founder might be how they would absorb that cost.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.180
Here's Stephen's take.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.620
You would think no one is dumb enough to think data centers are campuses, but I don't.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.900
Our second story, and the last of the main ones, moves from who writes the rules to who finds the holes.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:02.520
Yesterday, the robot report covered a report from VicOne, a company that sells security tooling.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:09.480
VicOne says A-assisted research found 28 zero-day findings in robotics software in under two days.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:15.520
The affected projects include ones developed or maintained by Google, NVIDIA, and Meta.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.380
Two caveats come from VicOne itself.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:25.200
The classification is its own initial validation, and the count may change after vendor review.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.200
On the evidence, this one is overstated. It is a vendor counting its own unconfirmed findings.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:37.220
There are no CVEs and no project names, and the report is followed by a product pitch.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.740
So our view, for now, is that 28 unnamed, unconfirmed findings from a vendor selling

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.660
the remedy read as a sales number. The underlying trend still matters, though.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:55.380
Cheap AI bug hunting does make upstream patch cadence a diligence line for robotics companies

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:01.580
built on Google, NVIDIA, or Metacode. We would want to know how quickly a startup picks up fixes

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:07.860
from the projects it depends on. For early-stage investors, there is a second angle. Robotics

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:14.120
startups built on that software inherit those projects bugs. That may open a wedge for robotics

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:20.660
specific security tooling, a market where VicOne already sells. There is a more forward-leaning

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:27.520
way to read the same facts. On the AGI forward reading, finding 28 candidate zero days in under

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:34.000
two days shows AI offense scaling now. Even if many fall away under vendor review, that argument

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.540
goes, the cost of discovering bugs has collapsed. The confirmed count, once vendors finish their

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:48.680
review, is what would test it. Here's Stephen's take, I for 1 AM keeping an eye on my Erebot.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:57.840
Now three quick hits all about money. First, Ottawa has committed $1.6 billion CAD in equity

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.680
to Cohere. It is likely the government's largest ever single private tech stake,

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.500
roughly triple the Canadian Tech Growth Fund.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.960
It sits inside a larger Series E that is planned, not closed,

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.920
so the close is what to watch.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.780
Second, a story we first covered last Friday.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.080
NVIDIA is in talks to provide billions of dollars in credit guarantees

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:25.760
for Nscale's $45 billion Anthropic Data Center project,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.400
a 460-megawatt facility.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.400
It matters because a chip vendor would be backing its customers' debt.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.960
Third, DeepSeq doubled its latest fundraising target to nearly $16 billion.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:44.520
Backers include Tencent, CATL, and Chinese state-owned institutions.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:50.520
That comes as black market prices for NVIDIA's advanced AI chips surged,

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.240
so capital is still reaching a Chinese lab even as chips get dearer.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.840
Stevens take on the quick hits.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.200
They ain't the sharpest knives in the drawer.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:26.760
One question worth asking in diligence this week. If a provider's financing rests on a vendor guarantee, what are the second provider and fallback model? And one to watch. Does NVIDIA formally guarantee credit for that N-Scale project and on what terms? A signed guarantee disclosed by NVIDIA or N-Scale or an Anthropic filing update would settle it.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:49.060
That's the briefing. Stay with me. Right after this, Bella takes you through today's deep dive on world models, simulators that learned by watching. You can also read it or listen to it on its own on the AI Briefing webpage, where you'll find the written edition in every past episode. The link is in the show notes.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:59.640
thanks michael this is the ai briefing deep dive for monday november 2 world models simulators that

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:06.200
learned by watching i'm bella like the briefing it is written and read by ai from public sources

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:14.720
in the last two weeks two launches use the same phrase on october 17 odyssey posted a video of a

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.980
simulated fruit fly brain flying through a world its model generated on the fly.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:25.020
Two days later, Skyfall AI released Sky One, which it calls an engineering world model for

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:30.020
computer-aided design and building plans. Behind Waymo's new driving simulator sits

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:34.920
Google DeepMind's Ginny 3. Waymo has built its driving simulator on top of it.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:40.880
The money is serious too. In June, Odyssey raised a $310 million

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:48.560
Series B at a $1.45 billion valuation. Still, ask 10 investors what a world model is,

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:53.720
and you will hear 10 answers. Part of the reason is that the term now covers at least three

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:59.600
different things. So today we take the idea apart. What does a world model learn? How is it different

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.360
from a language model? And which of the claims around it have actually been shown?

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:10.660
Start with the difference. A language model predicts the next word. A world model predicts

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:15.100
the next moment, given an action. You tell it you turn left, and it shows what you would see

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:20.580
after turning left. Then it does that again, many times a second. Here is the analogy worth keeping.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:26.660
Pilots train in flight simulators because crashing one costs nothing. A traditional simulator is

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:31.740
hand-built. Engineers write the physics, model the runway, and code every gust of wind.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:36.200
A world model is a simulator that taught itself by watching.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.480
Odyssey says its model was trained on internet video,

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.240
gameplay recordings, and simulated physics.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:45.600
Nobody wrote down that cup's fall or that door's swing.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:48.180
The model picked up those patterns from footage,

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.580
the way a toddler learns gravity long before reading Newton.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.900
The catch is that this simulator works like a dream.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.080
Each moment looks believable next to the one before,

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.480
but nothing guarantees the rules hold.

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.200
Turn around and the street behind you may have changed.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.600
Leave the room and come back, and the furniture may have moved.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:09.920
Under one label sit three families.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:11.780
The first draws pixels.

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:17.160
Genie 3 generates each new frame in real time, at 24 frames per second,

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.060
so a person or an agent like DeepMind Syma can navigate inside the world it creates.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:25.320
The second predicts in shorthand rather than pictures.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.580
Meta's vGPA2 does not draw the future.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.120
It predicts a compressed summary of what comes next,

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.720
and that summary is enough for a robot to plan.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:38.460
Jan Lakun at Meta has long argued that this is the root that matters.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:40.900
The third family stretches the word.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.240
Sky One does not render a world at all.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.680
Its world is the state of a design file,

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.100
and it plans the sequence of modeling steps needed to build a part or a floor plan.

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:54.920
All three share one idea, learn how a state changes when you act on it.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.180
That is what lets you rehearse before you commit.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:01.820
A robot can try a grasp a thousand times in imagination.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.880
and a car can meet a flood it has never seen.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:09.140
With that map in hand, let's look at what has actually been shown.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:12.480
Start with what is solid.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:19.720
DeepMind says Genie 3 produces interactive worlds at 720p and 24 frames per second.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:23.420
It says they stay largely consistent for several minutes,

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:26.040
with visual memory going back about a minute.

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:29.120
That is DeepMind describing its own work.

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:38.360
But since January, paying subscribers on Google's $249 per month AI Ultra plan have been able to try it through Project Genie

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:41.240
So outsiders can now test the basic behavior

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:44.140
DeepMind also lists the limits itself

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:50.000
In its words, the model supports a few minutes of interaction rather than extended hours

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:55.680
An analysis on tech talks adds physics errors, such as people appearing to walk backward

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:00.060
When DeepMind put its game-playing agent inside Genie 3,

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:01.660
the agent could only move around.

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:03.440
It did not pick things up.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:08.680
Meta reports that VGPA 2 needed only 62 hours of robot video to learn to plan.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:14.280
It says zero-shot pick-and-play success in new settings ran from 65% to 80%.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:17.620
That is self-reported on Meta's own lab tasks.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:22.160
Waymo says its Genie-based model produces both camera and LiDAR data

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:25.760
and can stage tornadoes, floods, and even elephants.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:31.920
As the decoder noted, Waymo shared no benchmark results or independent evaluations.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:34.640
Now the claims that go beyond the evidence.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:37.740
The fly demo is a video on X, there is no paper.

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:41.200
Odyssey has not said which fly brain map it used.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:48.780
A public map called Flywire has 139,255 neurons and roughly 50 million connections.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:53.360
We don't know how the generated frames are turned into signals for the fly's eyes.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:58.060
We also don't know whether the virtual fly behaves anything like a real one.

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:02.860
For now, it is a striking demo from a company that has just raised a large round.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.240
SkyOne has a benchmark, but Skyfall built it.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:11.760
Every figure is self-reported, with no weights, no API, and no outside replication.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:13.240
That is a tie.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:18.120
One independent write-up reads the preview as producing sketches and visual states,

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:21.200
with editable design histories still future work.

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.480
The same facts support quite different readings.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:28.220
The AGI forward reading goes like this.

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:33.080
Robotics is short of data, and every real world trial is slow and costly.

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:36.040
A model that has absorbed physics from video

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:39.480
can generate endless practice worlds for agents and robots.

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:42.300
On this view, Genie 3's memory,

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:45.420
which DeepMind says it did not program explicitly,

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.220
hints at understanding emerging from scale.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:52.080
Waymo, which runs a commercial robotaxi fleet,

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:55.280
putting this into its pipeline counts as a serious vote.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:58.380
The AA realist reading starts from the dream problem.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:01.420
A few minutes of consistency is fine for a demo.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:03.160
It is not enough for training.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:07.240
Teach a robot inside a simulator that gets physics slightly wrong,

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:10.260
and it learns the wrong physics with confidence.

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:15.260
Robotics has wrestled with this gap between simulation and reality for decades.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:20.980
None of the sources we checked shows a robot or car trained mainly in generated worlds

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:23.240
outperforming one trained on real data.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:27.740
On that view, today's value is in testing and rare scenario coverage,

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:29.520
not in replacing real data.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:31.900
The market reading looks at who is paying.

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:36.600
Odyssey's backers include Amazon, AMD Ventures, and GV,

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.080
and a WS is its preferred cloud.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:41.920
Chip and cloud companies are funding a customer

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.920
that consumes a great deal of compute.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:51.280
That is a fair bet, but it means the valuation says as much about compute strategy as about demand.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.960
Odyssey has not disclosed revenue.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:15:00.400
Our read is that world models are real infrastructure for simulation and testing.

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:05.540
As a source of training data, they are still early, and in some launches, they are just a label.

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:10.080
Each reading would change with one piece of evidence, which we come to next.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:14.500
For builders, the general-purpose pixel model is a capital game,

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:17.720
and Google and well-funded labs are already playing it.

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:20.040
Serving cost matters as well.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:26.080
In 2025, Odyssey's earlier model streamed from clusters of H100GPS

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:28.780
at $1 to $2 per user hour.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:32.280
A video game runs on a console the player already owns.

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:35.560
A consumer product built on a hosted world model

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:39.540
has to cover that GPU bill every hour, for every player.

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:43.960
Seed and Series A companies seem better placed where the world is narrow,

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.680
and the output can be checked.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:48.920
A CAD part can be measured against a geometry engine.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:53.900
Owning interaction data, such as robot logs or sensor feeds,

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.880
gives a company something to fine-tune with that other's lack.

00:15:57.560 --> 00:15:59.560
Waymo's LiDAR step is the template here,

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:04.360
tooling that measures whether simulation results hold up in reality looks undersupplied.

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:08.340
The exposed companies are thin wrappers on a model someone else hosts.

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:11.760
One question worth exploring in diligence might be this.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:16.120
How often have the company's simulated results been checked against the real world,

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.080
and what happened when they disagreed?

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:22.060
Three things would settle a lot of this.

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:27.240
First, a published result from Waymo, DeepMind, or a robotics lab,

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:32.040
where training mainly in a generated world beats training on real data with numbers.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.000
That would move us towards the AGI forward reading.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:38.220
Its continued absence would support the realist one.

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:43.020
Second, outside testing of Sky One once the waitlist opens.

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:47.940
We would wanna know whether the cost gap holds on someone else's parts and

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:52.040
whether engineers get editable design files or just pictures.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:56.920
Third, a paper from Odyssey showing how the fly brain is wired into the model and

00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:59.540
whether the fly behaves like a fly.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:01.220
None of these has a date.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:05.140
Over the next year, falling serving costs per user hour will show which

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:07.300
consumer uses can pay their way.

00:17:08.340 --> 00:17:11.880
That's the deep dive and today's AI briefing.

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:14.620
Every source is linked in the show notes,

00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:17.820
and the AI briefing webpage has the written edition,

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:20.600
this deep dive and every past episode.

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:23.000
The daily briefing is back tomorrow.
